---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/global/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/global/Footer.astro';
import Container from '../../components/global/Container.astro';
import Section from '../../components/global/Section.astro';
import TechnicalLabel from '../../components/global/TechnicalLabel.astro';
import Button from '../../components/global/Button.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="When a Display Becomes a Computing Platform | AionSi" description="As mission-critical visualization platforms absorb FPGA processing, embedded compute, high-speed interfaces and AI, the engineering challenge is increasingly at the boundaries between them.">
  <Header />
  <main>
    <Section class="bg-[var(--aionsi-surface-alt)] py-14 md:py-20">
      <Container>
        <TechnicalLabel>ENGINEERING INSIGHT · FPGA · EMBEDDED · VERIFICATION</TechnicalLabel>
        <div class="mt-6 max-w-5xl">
          <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--aionsi-navy)] md:text-6xl">When a Display Becomes a Computing Platform: The Engineering Challenge Behind Next-Generation Visualization</h1>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-xl leading-9 text-slate-600 md:text-2xl">As visualization systems absorb more processing, AI, high-speed interfaces and real-time data, the difficult part is no longer simply displaying information. It is engineering the boundaries between compute, FPGA, software and verification.</p>
          <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-slate-500">
            <span class="border border-slate-200 bg-white px-3 py-2">Mission-critical systems</span>
            <span class="border border-slate-200 bg-white px-3 py-2">FPGA & RTL</span>
            <span class="border border-slate-200 bg-white px-3 py-2">Embedded compute</span>
            <span class="border border-slate-200 bg-white px-3 py-2">Verification</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </Section>

    <section class="border-y border-slate-200 bg-white">
      <Container>
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl py-10 md:py-14">
          <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:text-[var(--aionsi-navy)] prose-a:text-[var(--aionsi-blue)] prose-p:text-slate-600 prose-p:leading-8 prose-li:text-slate-600">
            <h2>A display is no longer just a display</h2>
            <p>For decades, the engineering objective for a mission-critical display was relatively straightforward: receive information, render it reliably and make it visible to the operator.</p>
            <p>That architecture is changing.</p>
            <p>Modern avionics, defense and air-traffic-control systems increasingly bring together high-resolution video, multiple sensor feeds, embedded computing, real-time processing, networking and intelligent data analysis.</p>
            <p>The display is becoming part of the computing architecture.</p>
            <p>And that creates an interesting engineering question:</p>
            <blockquote><strong>Where should the intelligence of the system live?</strong></blockquote>

            <h2>The real challenge is not choosing FPGA or CPU</h2>
            <p>It is deciding what belongs where.</p>
            <p>FPGA logic can provide deterministic processing, low latency and flexible hardware-based data paths.</p>
            <p>Embedded processors provide programmability, application processing and increasingly sophisticated AI capabilities.</p>
            <p>Software provides another layer of flexibility.</p>
            <p>The architecture becomes powerful when these elements work together. It also becomes much harder to verify.</p>
            <p>A seemingly simple video path can cross multiple boundaries:</p>
            <p><strong>Sensor → interface → FPGA → memory → processor → software → display</strong></p>
            <p>Every boundary introduces another opportunity for latency, synchronization, data integrity or integration problems.</p>
            <p>This is where engineering teams often discover that the hardest problems are not inside individual components.</p>
            <p><strong>They are between them.</strong></p>

            <h2>Three engineering questions worth asking early</h2>
            <h3>1. Which functions really need deterministic hardware processing?</h3>
            <p>Not every data-processing function belongs in the FPGA.</p>
            <p>At the same time, pushing too much functionality into software can introduce latency, processor load or unpredictable system behavior.</p>
            <p>The right architecture depends on the required throughput, latency, determinism and future upgrade requirements.</p>
            <p>That makes FPGA architecture an important system-level decision—not simply an RTL implementation exercise.</p>

            <h3>2. Where does verification stop and integration begin?</h3>
            <p>This boundary is becoming increasingly blurred.</p>
            <p>A block may pass simulation but still fail when connected to a high-speed interface, a different clock domain, embedded software, real sensor data or a system-level workload.</p>
            <p>For complex platforms, verification therefore has to move beyond isolated IP blocks.</p>
            <p>SystemVerilog/UVM environments, regression automation, coverage and requirements traceability become more valuable when they are connected to actual system behavior.</p>

            <h3>3. How do you verify the complete data path?</h3>
            <p>This may be the biggest challenge.</p>
            <p>Testing an FPGA block independently tells you something. Testing the processor independently tells you something else.</p>
            <p>But the operator does not experience either one independently.</p>
            <p>They experience the <strong>complete path</strong>.</p>
            <p>That means engineering teams increasingly need validation strategies that connect:</p>
            <p><strong>FPGA + interfaces + embedded compute + software + visualization</strong></p>
            <p>rather than treating them as separate engineering islands.</p>

            <h2>High-speed interfaces are becoming engineering boundaries</h2>
            <p>PCIe, Ethernet and other high-speed interfaces are not just communication mechanisms. They are architectural boundaries.</p>
            <p>They determine how quickly data moves, how systems synchronize, how errors are handled and how components behave under real workloads.</p>
            <p>As data volumes increase, these interfaces can become one of the most difficult areas to debug because an apparently small timing or protocol problem can surface much further downstream.</p>
            <p><strong>Interface engineering should be considered part of system architecture, not an integration task left until the end.</strong></p>

            <h2>AI adds another layer of complexity</h2>
            <p>The arrival of embedded AI makes the architecture even more interesting.</p>
            <p>Modern visualization and video-processing platforms increasingly combine FPGA-based processing with embedded CPU/GPU compute for high-performance and AI workloads.</p>
            <p>This creates a new engineering question:</p>
            <blockquote><strong>How should deterministic video processing and flexible AI processing work together?</strong></blockquote>
            <p>There is no universal answer.</p>
            <p>Some workloads naturally belong in FPGA logic. Others belong in GPU or CPU compute.</p>
            <p>The challenge is designing the handoff between them so that the system remains predictable, testable and maintainable.</p>

            <h2>The engineering model may need to change too</h2>
            <p>As architectures become more complicated, adding more engineers does not automatically solve the problem.</p>
            <p>The more useful question may be:</p>
            <blockquote><strong>Which technical problem needs clear ownership?</strong></blockquote>
            <p>A team may need someone to own FPGA architecture. Another group may need additional verification capacity. A program may have a difficult high-speed interface integration problem. Or perhaps the biggest issue is simply the growing amount of work between hardware and software teams.</p>
            <p>These are very different problems. They should not all be solved with the same engineering model.</p>
            <p>A focused engineering team taking ownership of a defined technical outcome can sometimes create more value than simply increasing headcount.</p>

            <h2>A thought for engineering leaders</h2>
            <p>As visualization platforms evolve into integrated compute platforms, the competitive advantage may increasingly come from <strong>how well the engineering disciplines connect</strong>.</p>
            <p>FPGA. Embedded compute. High-speed interfaces. Software. Verification. System integration.</p>
            <p>The strongest architecture is not necessarily the one with the most advanced individual technologies.</p>
            <p>It may be the one where those technologies are <strong>engineered to work together with the fewest surprises</strong>.</p>
            <p>And perhaps that is the more interesting question for the next generation of mission-critical visualization:</p>
            <blockquote><strong>Are we designing better components—or designing better connections between them?</strong></blockquote>
          </article>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </section>

    <Section class="bg-[var(--aionsi-surface-alt)]">
      <Container>
        <div class="grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[1.4fr_.6fr] lg:items-center">
          <div>
            <TechnicalLabel>ENGINEERING PERSPECTIVE</TechnicalLabel>
            <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold text-[var(--aionsi-navy)] md:text-4xl">What engineering boundary is slowing your program today?</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">AionSi works across FPGA, digital verification, high-speed interfaces and embedded systems—especially where the difficult work sits between disciplines.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="flex justify-start lg:justify-end">
            <Button href="/contact">Start an Engineering Conversation</Button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </Section>

    <Section>
      <Container>
        <div class="border border-slate-200 bg-white p-7 shadow-sm md:p-9">
          <TechnicalLabel>RELATED</TechnicalLabel>
          <h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--aionsi-navy)]">Explore AionSi’s engineering focus for mission-critical platforms.</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-7 text-slate-600">See how FPGA engineering, verification, high-speed interfaces and embedded integration can be brought together around a defined technical challenge.</p>
          <a href="/scioteq" class="mt-5 inline-flex font-semibold text-[var(--aionsi-blue)]">Explore the ScioTeq engineering focus →</a>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </Section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
